        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c024_q2", text: "Increased desire for independence during adolescence is:", options: ["A sign of disrespect only", "A normal part of developing identity and self-reliance", "Caused by poor parenting", "Only seen in boys"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Adolescents naturally seek independence as they develop their own identity — this is a normal and healthy developmental stage."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c024_q3", text: "Peer pressure during adolescence means:", options: ["Pressure from teachers only", "Influence from friends to behave in certain ways", "Pressure to study more", "Pressure from parents"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Peer pressure is the influence friends have on each other's choices — it can be positive (study together) or negative (risky behaviour)."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c024_q4", text: "Which is a healthy way to handle strong emotions during adolescence?", options: ["Suppressing all feelings", "Talking to a trusted adult, exercising or journaling", "Acting impulsively always", "Avoiding all social contact"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Healthy emotional management includes expressing feelings constructively — talking, creative outlets and exercise all help."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c024_q5", text: "Self-consciousness in adolescence (worrying about appearance) is:", options: ["Unique to just one culture", "Common worldwide due to physical changes and increased social awareness", "A mental illness", "Only experienced by girls"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Physical changes and increased social awareness make adolescents more conscious of appearance — this is universal and normal.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c025: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c025_q1", text: "Which habit most supports well-being during adolescence?", options: ["Staying up very late every night", "Regular physical exercise, balanced diet and adequate sleep", "Spending all free time on screens", "Skipping meals to stay thin"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Exercise supports physical and mental health; a balanced diet provides nutrients for growth; sleep is essential for brain development."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c025_q2", text: "Staying away from tobacco, alcohol and drugs during adolescence is important because:", options: ["They are legal for teens", "The developing brain is especially vulnerable to long-term harm from these substances", "Only older people are affected", "They improve confidence short-term with no side effects"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The adolescent brain is still developing — substances can disrupt this development, causing lasting harm to memory, mood and health."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c025_q3", text: "Having a supportive friend group during adolescence helps with:", options: ["Avoiding all challenges", "Emotional resilience and a sense of belonging", "Always agreeing with each other", "Ignoring problems"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Positive friendships provide support, reduce loneliness and help adolescents develop social skills and confidence."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c025_q4", text: "Pursuing a hobby during adolescence is beneficial because:", options: ["It wastes time", "It provides a positive outlet for energy, develops skills and reduces stress", "Only academic subjects matter", "Hobbies are only for adults"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Hobbies build skills, provide enjoyment and serve as a healthy outlet — they can also support mental well-being."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c025_q5", text: "Seeking help from a trusted adult when troubled is:", options: ["A sign of weakness", "A smart, healthy choice that helps resolve problems early", "Not necessary for teenagers", "Only for young children"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Talking to parents, teachers or counsellors helps adolescents navigate challenges — it's a sign of self-awareness, not weakness.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c026: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c026_q1", text: "Adolescence exists in humans because:", options: ["It is just a cultural invention", "The human brain and body need time to mature before reproduction — a longer childhood for a complex species", "Adolescence is accidental and has no purpose", "Only humans eat complex food"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Human brains are exceptionally complex — the extended adolescence allows time for brain development, learning and social skill building."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c026_q2", text: "The hormones that trigger puberty are released by:", options: ["The stomach", "The pituitary gland signalling the gonads (ovaries/testes)", "The liver", "The kidneys"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The pituitary gland releases FSH and LH hormones that signal the gonads to produce sex hormones, starting puberty."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c026_q3", text: "The purpose of physical changes during adolescence is primarily:", options: ["To make teenagers taller only", "To prepare the body for reproduction and adulthood", "To improve memory and study skills", "To make the body stronger for sports only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The physical changes of puberty prepare the reproductive system — they are biological preparations for adulthood and potential reproduction."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c026_q4", text: "Adolescence is considered a sensitive developmental period because:", options: ["Teenagers are more emotional than adults", "Rapid changes in body and brain make this period influential for lifelong health and behaviour patterns", "Schools are harder during this time", "Teenagers need more sleep than any other age group"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Brain development during adolescence shapes identity, decision-making patterns and mental health — influences in this period have lasting effects."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c026_q5", text: "Nutrition is especially important during adolescence because:", options: ["Teenagers are always hungry", "Rapid growth and development require extra nutrients — calcium, iron and protein are especially needed", "Adults need more nutrients than teens", "Diet only affects weight during adolescence"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Growing bones need calcium; blood production needs iron; muscle development needs protein — nutritional needs peak during the adolescent growth spurt.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c027: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c027_q1", text: "Heat conduction is the transfer of heat:", options: ["Through empty space as waves", "Through a liquid by circular currents", "Through direct contact between particles in a solid", "Only in gases"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "In conduction, heat is passed from particle to particle by direct collision — it works best in solids where particles are close together."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c027_q2", text: "Metals are good conductors of heat because:", options: ["They are shiny", "They have free electrons and tightly packed atoms that transfer vibrations quickly", "They are heavy", "They are rigid"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Free electrons in metals carry thermal energy rapidly, making metals far better heat conductors than non-metals."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c027_q3", text: "Wooden handles on cooking pots help because wood is:", options: ["A good heat conductor", "A poor heat conductor (insulator) — it stays cool while the pot is hot", "Magnetic", "Waterproof"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Wood is a thermal insulator — heat does not pass through it easily, so the handle stays comfortable to touch."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c027_q4", text: "In which medium does heat conduction occur MOST efficiently?", options: ["Gases", "Liquids", "Vacuum", "Solids (especially metals)"], correctIndex: 3, explanation: "Solids have closely packed particles — vibrations and free electron movement conduct heat most efficiently in dense metal solids."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c027_q5", text: "Why do woollen clothes keep us warm?", options: ["Wool generates its own heat", "Wool traps air, and trapped air is a poor conductor — it reduces heat loss from the body", "Wool absorbs heat from the sun", "Wool is a metal-like conductor"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Wool fibres trap air pockets — still air is a very poor heat conductor, so body heat is retained inside the clothing.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c028: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c028_q1", text: "Convection is heat transfer that occurs through:", options: ["Direct contact in solids", "Circular movement of fluid (liquid or gas) particles", "Electromagnetic waves in a vacuum", "Particle vibration only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "In convection, heated fluid rises (less dense) and cooler fluid sinks — this circular movement (convection current) transfers heat."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c028_q2", text: "When water is heated from below, convection currents form because:", options: ["Hot water sinks and cold water rises", "Hot water rises (less dense) and cool water sinks (denser), creating circulation", "Water stays still and conducts heat", "Bubbles carry heat upwards only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Heated water expands and becomes less dense, so it rises. Cooler denser water sinks to take its place — continuous circular flow."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c028_q3", text: "Sea breezes are caused by convection because:", options: ["The sea is always cooler than land", "Land heats up faster — hot air rises over land, cooler sea air moves in to replace it", "Wind only blows from one direction", "The sea reflects sunlight better"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Land heats faster than sea during the day — rising warm air over land creates low pressure, pulling cool sea air inland as a sea breeze."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c028_q4", text: "Convection occurs in which mediums?", options: ["Only solids", "Only gases", "Liquids and gases (fluids)", "Only vacuum"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Convection requires particles that can flow and move — liquids and gases are fluids that can form convection currents. Solids cannot."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c028_q5", text: "A room heater placed near the floor heats the room more effectively because:", options: ["Heat only radiates from the floor", "Hot air rises — convection currents distribute the warm air throughout the room", "Cold air stays at the bottom always", "Floor placement maximises radiation"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Heaters near the floor warm the air; warm air rises; cool air near the ceiling sinks to be heated — convection distributes heat throughout the room.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c029: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c029_q1", text: "Radiation transfers heat:", options: ["Only through solids", "Only through liquids", "Through electromagnetic waves — no medium needed", "Only through air"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Radiation is heat transfer by electromagnetic waves (infrared) — it can travel through a vacuum. This is how the Sun heats Earth."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c029_q2", text: "The Sun heats Earth by:", options: ["Conduction — direct contact", "Convection — air currents from the Sun", "Radiation — electromagnetic waves travelling through space", "All three equally"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Space is a vacuum — conduction and convection need a medium. The Sun heats Earth purely through radiation (infrared waves)."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c029_q3", text: "Dark-coloured surfaces are better absorbers of radiation because:", options: ["They reflect all radiation", "Dark surfaces absorb more radiant energy and heat up faster", "Dark colours produce their own heat", "They conduct heat from surroundings"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Dark (matte) surfaces are better absorbers and emitters of infrared radiation — they heat up more quickly in sunlight."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c029_q4", text: "Shiny, light-coloured surfaces are used in hot climates because they:", options: ["Absorb maximum radiation", "Reflect most radiation, keeping buildings and people cooler", "Conduct heat inward", "Improve convection indoors"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Shiny and white surfaces reflect infrared radiation — less heat is absorbed, keeping interiors and clothing cooler in hot weather."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c029_q5", text: "A thermos flask keeps drinks hot by:", options: ["Generating its own heat", "Reducing all three modes of heat transfer — silvered walls reflect radiation, vacuum eliminates conduction and convection", "Conducting heat from the environment", "Using convection currents inside"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A thermos (vacuum flask) has a double wall with vacuum between (no conduction/convection) and silvered surfaces (reflect radiation) — excellent insulator.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c030: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c030_q1", text: "The water cycle is driven mainly by:", options: ["Wind energy", "Solar energy (from the Sun)", "Tidal energy", "Geothermal energy"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The Sun's energy evaporates water from oceans, lakes and rivers — this drives the entire water cycle."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c030_q2", text: "Evaporation in the water cycle converts water into:", options: ["Ice", "Liquid water stored in rivers", "Water vapour that rises into the atmosphere", "Soil moisture only"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Solar energy converts liquid water to water vapour — this invisible gas rises into the atmosphere where it eventually cools and condenses."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c030_q3", text: "Clouds form in the water cycle during:", options: ["Evaporation", "Condensation — water vapour cools and forms tiny water droplets", "Precipitation", "Transpiration"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "As water vapour rises and cools, it condenses around dust particles forming tiny droplets — billions of these form clouds."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c030_q4", text: "Transpiration in plants contributes to the water cycle by:", options: ["Adding CO₂ to air", "Releasing water vapour from leaves into the atmosphere", "Absorbing rainfall only", "Cooling the soil"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Plants absorb water through roots and release it as vapour through leaf pores (stomata) — contributing significantly to atmospheric moisture."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c030_q5", text: "Groundwater gets replenished when:", options: ["Ice melts in rivers only", "Rainfall seeps through soil and rocks into underground aquifers", "Clouds move over land", "Evaporation occurs from oceans"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Infiltration is rainwater soaking into the ground — it refills underground water reserves (aquifers) that supply wells and springs.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c031: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c031_q1", text: "The SI unit of time is:", options: ["Minute", "Hour", "Second", "Millisecond"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "The second (s) is the standard international unit of time — all other time units are multiples or fractions of the second."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c031_q2", text: "A simple pendulum can measure time because:", options: ["It swings randomly", "Its time period (one complete swing) is constant for a given length", "It generates electricity", "It measures temperature changes"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A pendulum's time period depends on its length (not swing size) — this regularity makes it a reliable time-keeping device."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c031_q3", text: "Ancient civilisations used which natural events to measure time?", options: ["Speed of rivers", "Movement of the Sun, Moon and stars (day, month, year)", "Amount of rainfall", "Growth of plants"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Day/night (Earth's rotation), lunar cycles (month), and Earth's revolution (year) are natural repeating events used as early clocks and calendars."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c031_q4", text: "One day is defined as:", options: ["The time from sunrise to sunset", "The time for Earth to complete one full rotation on its axis (approximately 24 hours)", "The time for Moon to orbit Earth once", "12 hours of sunlight"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "One day = one full rotation of Earth on its axis = 24 hours. Sunrise to sunset is only the daylight half."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c031_q5", text: "Modern atomic clocks are more accurate than mechanical clocks because:", options: ["They use larger pendulums", "They measure vibrations of atoms (billions per second) — extremely precise and consistent", "They run on solar energy", "They are made of gold"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Atomic clocks use the ultra-stable vibration frequency of atoms (like caesium) — accurate to within a second in millions of years.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c032: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c032_q1", text: "The speed of an object is defined as:", options: ["How far it travels", "Distance travelled divided by time taken", "Time taken to travel", "Direction of movement"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Speed = Distance ÷ Time. It tells us how quickly an object covers distance, without specifying direction."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c032_q2", text: "If two cars travel the same distance but one takes less time, which is faster?", options: ["The one that takes more time", "The one that takes less time", "They are equally fast", "Cannot be determined without direction"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Speed = Distance/Time — same distance, less time means greater speed. Dividing by a smaller number gives a bigger result."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c032_q3", text: "A car covers 120 km in 2 hours. Its speed is:", options: ["240 km/h", "60 km/h", "120 km/h", "30 km/h"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Speed = 120 km ÷ 2 h = 60 km/h. This is average speed — the car may have gone faster or slower at different points."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c032_q4", text: "The unit of speed in SI is:", options: ["km/h", "m/min", "m/s (metres per second)", "cm/s only"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "The SI unit of speed is metres per second (m/s) — though km/h is commonly used for vehicles."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c032_q5", text: "Average speed over a journey is calculated using:", options: ["Only the fastest speed reached", "Total distance ÷ total time for the whole journey", "Distance of each section separately", "Only the slowest speed"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Average speed = total distance / total time — it gives a single representative value even if the speed varied throughout the journey.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c033: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c033_q1", text: "Speed tells us about motion but NOT:", options: ["How fast an object moves", "The direction of motion (that requires velocity)", "The distance covered per unit time", "How quickly distance changes"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Speed only tells how fast — velocity adds direction. A car going 60 km/h north and 60 km/h south have the same speed but different velocities."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c033_q2", text: "A bus travels 300 km in 5 hours. What is its average speed?", options: ["60 km/h", "1500 km/h", "50 km/h", "300 km/h"], correctIndex: 0, explanation: "Speed = 300 ÷ 5 = 60 km/h. The bus covers 60 kilometres every hour on average."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c033_q3", text: "If speed increases while time stays the same, the distance covered:", options: ["Stays the same", "Decreases", "Increases", "Becomes zero"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Distance = Speed × Time — if speed increases and time is constant, distance increases proportionally."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c033_q4", text: "A cheetah can run at 110 km/h. A car travels at 80 km/h. In a 1-km race, which reaches the finish first?", options: ["The car — it is more reliable", "The cheetah — it has a higher speed over short distances", "They arrive at the same time", "Cannot be determined"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Higher speed means covering the same distance in less time — the cheetah at 110 km/h would finish before the car at 80 km/h."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c033_q5", text: "The speedometer of a vehicle shows:", options: ["Average speed for the whole journey", "The instantaneous speed at that moment", "Distance covered", "Direction of travel"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A speedometer shows instantaneous speed — how fast the vehicle is moving at that exact instant, not an average.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c034: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c034_q1", text: "Uniform linear motion means an object:", options: ["Changes speed continuously", "Moves in a straight line at constant speed", "Moves in a circle", "Slows down gradually"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Uniform linear motion = constant speed + straight-line path. Equal distances are covered in equal time intervals."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c034_q2", text: "Non-uniform motion means:", options: ["Speed is always constant", "The object moves in a circle only", "Speed changes — unequal distances in equal time intervals", "The object moves backwards"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Non-uniform motion has varying speed — a car accelerating in traffic covers different distances in each second."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c034_q3", text: "A distance-time graph of uniform motion appears as:", options: ["A curve", "A horizontal line", "A straight diagonal line (constant slope)", "A zigzag"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Constant speed means equal distance per unit time — the distance-time graph is a straight line with constant positive slope."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c034_q4", text: "On a distance-time graph, a steeper line means:", options: ["Slower speed", "Greater speed (more distance covered per unit time)", "The object has stopped", "The object is accelerating only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Steeper slope = greater rise (distance) per run (time) = higher speed. A flatter line means slower speed."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c034_q5", text: "A horizontal line on a distance-time graph means the object is:", options: ["Moving at constant speed", "Accelerating", "At rest — no distance change over time", "Moving backwards"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "A horizontal line means distance is not changing — the object is stationary. Zero slope = zero speed.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c035: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c035_q1", text: "Ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation and egestion are steps of:", options: ["Respiration", "Nutrition in animals", "Photosynthesis", "Excretion"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "These five steps describe how animals take in food, break it down, absorb nutrients, use them and remove waste."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c035_q2", text: "Digestion of carbohydrates begins in the:", options: ["Stomach", "Small intestine", "Mouth — saliva contains amylase", "Large intestine"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Salivary amylase in the mouth begins breaking down starch (a carbohydrate) into simpler sugars even before food reaches the stomach."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c035_q3", text: "The main site for digestion and absorption of nutrients in humans is the:", options: ["Stomach", "Large intestine", "Small intestine", "Mouth"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "The small intestine is where most digestion is completed (by pancreatic and intestinal enzymes) and nutrients are absorbed into blood."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c035_q4", text: "The stomach's hydrochloric acid serves to:", options: ["Digest fats", "Digest starch", "Kill bacteria in food and create an acidic environment for pepsin to work", "Absorb vitamins"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "HCl sterilises food and provides the low pH needed for pepsin (protein-digesting enzyme) to work effectively in the stomach."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c035_q5", text: "Villi in the small intestine increase:", options: ["The length of the intestine", "Surface area for efficient nutrient absorption into the blood", "Acid production", "Enzyme storage"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Villi are finger-like projections — they dramatically increase the absorptive surface area, allowing nutrients to pass rapidly into the bloodstream.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c036: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c036_q1", text: "Grass-eating animals (like cows) have a long digestive system because:", options: ["They eat more food", "Cellulose in grass is hard to digest and requires more time and length to break down", "They need to store more food", "Their stomach produces less acid"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Cellulose digestion requires special bacteria and a lengthy system — ruminants have multi-chambered stomachs and long intestines."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c036_q2", text: "Ruminants (like cows) re-chew their food in a process called:", options: ["Regurgitation for waste removal", "Rumination — swallowed food is brought back to the mouth and re-chewed", "Assimilation", "Ingestion only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Ruminants swallow food quickly, then regurgitate (bring back) partially digested cud to chew thoroughly — maximising cellulose breakdown."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c036_q3", text: "Amoeba digests food using:", options: ["A mouth and stomach", "Food vacuoles — enzymes inside digest engulfed food particles", "Roots that absorb nutrients", "Chemical secretions from the surface"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Amoeba engulfs food by forming pseudopodia, creating a food vacuole — digestive enzymes inside the vacuole break down the food."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c036_q4", text: "The process by which Amoeba captures food is called:", options: ["Absorption", "Phagocytosis — engulfing particles by extending pseudopodia", "Filtration", "Osmosis"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Phagocytosis (cell eating) — Amoeba extends its pseudopodia to surround and engulf food particles, forming a food vacuole."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c036_q5", text: "The bacteria in the stomach of grass-eaters help by:", options: ["Making food more acidic", "Producing enzymes (cellulase) that break down cellulose", "Absorbing sunlight", "Producing vitamins only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Ruminants cannot produce cellulase — symbiotic bacteria in their stomach produce this enzyme, enabling cellulose digestion.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c037: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c037_q1", text: "All animals respire to:", options: ["Produce food", "Release energy from food molecules for life processes", "Remove CO₂ only", "Absorb oxygen for storage"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Respiration breaks down glucose using oxygen to release energy — this energy powers all cellular activities."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c037_q2", text: "The gases exchanged during respiration are:", options: ["Nitrogen in, CO₂ out", "Oxygen in, carbon dioxide out (and water vapour)", "CO₂ in, oxygen out", "Only oxygen is involved"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Animals breathe in oxygen (O₂) used in respiration and breathe out carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water vapour as waste products."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c037_q3", text: "Fish breathe underwater using:", options: ["Lungs", "Skin only", "Gills — they extract dissolved oxygen from water", "Air sacs"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Gills have feathery filaments with extensive blood supply — water flows over them and oxygen diffuses into the blood."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c037_q4", text: "Earthworms breathe through:", options: ["Lungs", "Gills", "Moist skin (cutaneous respiration)", "Spiracles"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Earthworms lack specialised respiratory organs — oxygen and CO₂ diffuse through their moist skin directly into and out of the blood."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c037_q5", text: "Insects breathe through tiny openings called:", options: ["Gills", "Nostrils", "Spiracles connected to a tracheal system", "Lungs"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Insects have spiracles (small pores on the body wall) connected to a network of tracheae that carry air directly to body cells.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c038: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c038_q1", text: "Plants grow by:", options: ["Only increasing in width", "Cell division and cell elongation, adding new cells at growing regions", "Absorbing soil and becoming heavier", "Only producing new leaves"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Plant growth occurs at meristems (growing tips) — cells divide rapidly, then elongate, adding height and new organs."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c038_q2", text: "The primary regions of growth in a plant are:", options: ["The middle of the stem only", "Root tips and shoot tips (apical meristems)", "Old woody regions", "The flowers only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Apical (tip) meristems in roots and shoots contain rapidly dividing cells — they are where most growth occurs in young plants."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c038_q3", text: "Germination of a seed begins when:", options: ["The seed gets sunlight directly", "The seed absorbs water and dormancy is broken, allowing the embryo to grow", "Temperature drops suddenly", "The seed is eaten and passes through an animal"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Water activates enzymes in the seed, breaks dormancy and triggers the embryo to resume growth — the root (radicle) emerges first."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c038_q4", text: "Plants respond to gravity (grow roots downward, shoots upward) due to:", options: ["Soil temperature", "Auxins — plant hormones that redistribute growth in response to gravity", "Wind direction", "Water availability only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Auxins are plant growth hormones that accumulate unevenly in response to gravity, directing root growth downward and shoot growth upward."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c038_q5", text: "Annual plants complete their life cycle in:", options: ["More than 2 years", "Exactly 10 years", "One growing season (one year)", "Just a few days"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Annual plants germinate, grow, flower, produce seeds and die all within a single year — wheat, marigold and sunflower are examples.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c039: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c039_q1", text: "Photosynthesis produces:", options: ["Carbon dioxide and water", "Glucose and oxygen", "Starch and carbon dioxide", "Nitrogen and glucose"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Photosynthesis: CO₂ + H₂O + light energy → glucose (food) + O₂. Plants produce glucose to use as energy and oxygen as a by-product."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c039_q2", text: "The green pigment in leaves that captures sunlight for photosynthesis is:", options: ["Carotene", "Chlorophyll", "Anthocyanin", "Melanin"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Chlorophyll gives leaves their green colour and absorbs red and blue light — the energy is used to drive photosynthesis."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c039_q3", text: "Insectivorous plants like the pitcher plant supplement their nutrition by:", options: ["Absorbing extra CO₂", "Trapping and digesting insects to obtain nitrogen compounds", "Photosynthesising more than normal plants", "Absorbing nutrients from other plant roots"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Insectivorous plants grow in nitrogen-poor soils — they trap insects and digest them to obtain nitrogen for making proteins."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c039_q4", text: "Parasitic plants like Cuscuta (dodder) get nutrition by:", options: ["Photosynthesis from their own leaves", "Absorbing food from a host plant through haustoria (sucking roots)", "Decomposing dead matter", "Storing sunlight in their stems"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Cuscuta lacks chlorophyll — it wraps around host plants and penetrates them with haustoria to steal water and nutrients."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c039_q5", text: "In photosynthesis, the oxygen released comes from:", options: ["CO₂ splitting", "Splitting of water molecules by light energy", "Glucose breaking down", "Chlorophyll reacting with air"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "During the light reactions, water molecules are split (photolysis) — the oxygen released is the by-product of this water-splitting step.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c040: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c040_q1", text: "Water and minerals are transported from roots to leaves through:", options: ["Phloem", "Xylem — hollow tube-like vessels", "Cortex cells", "Stomata"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Xylem vessels form a continuous pipeline from roots to leaves, transporting water and dissolved minerals upward."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c040_q2", text: "Food (glucose) made in leaves is transported to other parts of the plant through:", options: ["Xylem", "Phloem — sieve tubes", "Roots directly", "Air spaces in the stem"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Phloem transports sugars (mainly sucrose) from leaves (source) to roots, fruits and other plant parts (sinks) in all directions."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c040_q3", text: "The upward movement of water in tall trees is partly driven by:", options: ["The heart pumping water", "Transpiration pull — evaporation of water from leaves creates a suction force", "Gravity pulling water up", "Root pressure only"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Water evaporating from leaves (transpiration) pulls a continuous water column up through the xylem — like sucking through a very long straw."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c040_q4", text: "Osmosis in plant roots drives water uptake because:", options: ["Roots have more water than soil", "Root cells have higher solute concentration — water moves in by osmosis", "Roots pump water mechanically", "Gravity pulls water into roots"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Root cells have dissolved salts/minerals — their solution is more concentrated than soil water, so water enters by osmosis (from dilute to concentrated through semi-permeable membrane)."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c040_q5", text: "Transpiration (water loss from leaves) is important because:", options: ["It has no benefit for the plant", "It drives water transport up the plant and cools the leaf surface", "It removes excess food", "It prevents photosynthesis"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Transpiration serves two key roles: it pulls water up the xylem from roots to leaves, and the evaporation cools the leaf, preventing overheating.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c041: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c041_q1", text: "Do plants respire?", options: ["No — they only photosynthesise", "Yes — all living plant cells respire continuously, day and night", "Only at night", "Only the roots respire"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "All living plant cells need energy for growth and life processes — they respire 24 hours a day, breaking down glucose to release energy."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c041_q2", text: "During the day, a plant appears to only release oxygen because:", options: ["Respiration stops in daylight", "Photosynthesis produces more oxygen than respiration uses — the net release is oxygen", "Respiration slows down in light", "Stomata close during the day"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Both occur simultaneously during daytime — but photosynthesis (oxygen producer) outpaces respiration (oxygen consumer), so plants release net oxygen."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c041_q3", text: "At night, plants release:", options: ["Only oxygen", "Oxygen and nitrogen", "Carbon dioxide (respiration continues but photosynthesis stops)", "No gases at all"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Without light, photosynthesis stops — only respiration continues, consuming oxygen and releasing CO₂, same as animals."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c041_q4", text: "Gas exchange in leaves occurs through tiny pores called:", options: ["Villi", "Xylem pores", "Stomata (surrounded by guard cells)", "Lenticels only"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Stomata are small pores on leaf surfaces — they open and close (controlled by guard cells) to allow CO₂ in and O₂ out during photosynthesis."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c041_q5", text: "The compensation point is when:", options: ["Photosynthesis completely stops", "Rate of photosynthesis equals rate of respiration — no net gas exchange", "Respiration equals transpiration", "The plant stops growing"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "At the compensation point (usually at low light), O₂ produced by photosynthesis exactly equals O₂ consumed by respiration — there is no net exchange.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c042: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c042_q1", text: "Objects that produce their own light are called:", options: ["Reflectors", "Translucent objects", "Luminous objects", "Opaque objects"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Luminous objects emit their own light — the Sun, a flame, a light bulb and glowworms are luminous sources."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c042_q2", text: "The Moon appears bright at night because:", options: ["It produces its own light", "It reflects sunlight towards Earth", "It is close to Earth", "It generates electricity"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The Moon is a non-luminous object — it has no light of its own. It reflects sunlight, which is why it is visible at night."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c042_q3", text: "Which of these is a natural luminous source?", options: ["Candle", "Electric bulb", "The Sun", "Mirror"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "The Sun is the primary natural luminous source — it produces light through nuclear fusion reactions in its core."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c042_q4", text: "A torch is which type of light source?", options: ["Natural and luminous", "Artificial and luminous (produces its own light)", "Natural and non-luminous", "Artificial and non-luminous"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A torch is an artificial light source (man-made) and luminous — the bulb filament produces light by converting electrical energy."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c042_q5", text: "Bioluminescence (light from fireflies and deep-sea creatures) is:", options: ["Reflected moonlight", "Electrical discharge in the body", "Light produced by chemical reactions within living organisms", "Sunlight stored during the day"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Bioluminescence involves chemical reactions (luciferin + oxygen) inside the organism that release energy as visible light.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c043: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c043_q1", text: "Light travels in:", options: ["Curved paths always", "Straight lines (rectilinear propagation)", "Wavy paths like water", "Different paths depending on colour"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Light travels in straight lines — this is why shadows have sharp edges and why we cannot see around corners."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c043_q2", text: "The evidence that light travels in straight lines is:", options: ["Light can be seen through walls", "Shadows form because light cannot bend around opaque objects", "Light travels at different speeds", "Mirrors reflect light at various angles"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "If light bent around objects, no shadows would form — sharp shadow edges prove that light travels in straight lines from the source."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c043_q3", text: "A pinhole camera works because:", options: ["Light bends through the hole", "Light travels in straight lines through the tiny hole, forming an inverted image", "The hole collects all light and focuses it", "A lens is hidden in the hole"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Light from the top of an object travels straight through the pinhole to the bottom of the screen, and vice versa — creating an inverted image."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c043_q4", text: "In which medium does light travel fastest?", options: ["Water", "Glass", "Vacuum (empty space)", "Air"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Light travels at 300,000 km/s in a vacuum — it slows down when passing through any medium (water, glass, air). The vacuum is fastest."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c043_q5", text: "Eclipses (solar and lunar) are proof that:", options: ["The Sun is much larger than the Moon", "Light travels in straight lines — the Moon or Earth casts a shadow in a straight line", "Light can bend around spherical bodies", "Earth is flat"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "During an eclipse, the shadow cast by the Moon or Earth falls in a straight line — confirming that light does not bend around spherical objects.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c044: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c044_q1", text: "A transparent material:", options: ["Allows no light through", "Allows light through but scatters it", "Allows light to pass through clearly, so objects can be seen distinctly", "Reflects all light"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Transparent materials (clear glass, water) let light pass straight through — objects viewed through them appear clear and undistorted."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c044_q2", text: "A translucent material:", options: ["Blocks all light", "Allows some light through but scatters it — objects appear blurry", "Lets light through perfectly like glass", "Reflects all light back"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Translucent materials (frosted glass, thin cloth, tissue paper) let light through but scatter it, so objects appear hazy, not clear."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c044_q3", text: "An opaque material:", options: ["Lets all light through", "Lets some light through", "Blocks all light — no image can be seen through it", "Only reflects coloured light"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Opaque materials (wood, metal, brick) absorb or reflect all incident light — no light passes through, so objects behind them cannot be seen."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c044_q4", text: "Frosted glass in bathroom windows is used because it is:", options: ["Stronger than clear glass", "Translucent — it allows light in but prevents clear visibility from outside", "Opaque and blocks all light", "Transparent and perfectly clear"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Frosted glass scatters light (translucent) — it allows natural light into the bathroom while preventing outsiders from seeing clearly through it."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c044_q5", text: "Which is transparent?", options: ["Wood", "Frosted glass", "Clear water in a glass", "Cardboard"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Clear water allows light to pass through undistorted — you can see objects clearly through it, making it transparent.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c045: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c045_q1", text: "A shadow forms when:", options: ["Light passes through an object", "An opaque object blocks light, creating a dark region behind it", "A transparent object absorbs light", "Light bends around the object"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Shadows form when opaque objects block straight-travelling light — the region that receives no light is the shadow."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c045_q2", text: "The shadow of an object is always:", options: ["The same colour as the object", "Coloured — matching the object", "Black (or very dark) — it is the absence of light", "White always"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "A shadow is formed by the absence of light — regardless of the object's colour, the shadow is always dark (black or grey)."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c045_q3", text: "Moving a light source closer to an opaque object makes the shadow:", options: ["Smaller and clearer", "The same size", "Larger (umbra expands when the source is closer)", "Disappear"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Bringing the light source nearer to an opaque object enlarges the shadow — more light rays are blocked over a wider angle."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c045_q4", text: "A shadow is shortest when the Sun is:", options: ["On the horizon at sunrise", "Setting in the west", "Directly overhead (noon)", "Below the horizon"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "At solar noon, the Sun is directly above — shadows fall directly below objects, making them smallest. Shadows lengthen as the Sun lowers."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c045_q5", text: "Why can't a transparent object form a clear shadow?", options: ["It is too small", "Light passes through it — no light is blocked, so no shadow forms", "Its shadow is white", "Transparent objects absorb all light"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Shadows require light to be blocked — transparent objects allow light through, so no dark region (shadow) forms behind them.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c046: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c046_q1", text: "The law of reflection states that the angle of incidence:", options: ["Is always 90°", "Is always smaller than the angle of reflection", "Equals the angle of reflection", "Is always larger than the angle of reflection"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "The angle of incidence (between incident ray and normal) always equals the angle of reflection — this is the fundamental law of reflection."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c046_q2", text: "A normal in optics is:", options: ["A typical, average ray", "A perpendicular line drawn to the mirror surface at the point of incidence", "The incident ray before hitting the mirror", "The reflected ray direction"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The normal is an imaginary line perpendicular (at 90°) to the mirror's surface at the exact point where light hits — angles are measured from the normal."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c046_q3", text: "Regular (specular) reflection occurs on:", options: ["Rough, uneven surfaces", "Smooth, flat surfaces like a mirror", "Translucent surfaces", "Black surfaces that absorb light"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Smooth surfaces reflect light in a single direction (regular reflection) — this is why mirrors form clear images. Rough surfaces scatter light (diffuse reflection)."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c046_q4", text: "We can see non-luminous objects because:", options: ["They produce their own light", "They reflect light from a source into our eyes", "Our eyes glow in the dark", "Non-luminous objects are transparent"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Non-luminous objects reflect light from luminous sources (like the Sun or a lamp) — the reflected light enters our eyes, allowing us to see them."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c046_q5", text: "A periscope uses which property of light?", options: ["Refraction", "Diffraction", "Reflection from plane mirrors", "Absorption"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "A periscope uses two plane mirrors at 45° — light reflects off the top mirror and then the bottom mirror, allowing you to see around obstacles.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c047: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c047_q1", text: "The image formed in a plane mirror is:", options: ["Real and inverted", "Virtual, erect (upright) and the same size as the object", "Smaller than the object", "Larger than the object always"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Plane mirrors form virtual images (cannot be projected on a screen), upright, same size, and as far behind the mirror as the object is in front."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c047_q2", text: "What does 'virtual image' mean?", options: ["The image can be projected on a screen", "The image appears to be behind the mirror but light rays don't actually meet there", "The image is created by lenses only", "The image changes colour"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Virtual images form where reflected rays appear to diverge from — they cannot be caught on a screen because no actual light rays pass through that point."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c047_q3", text: "Lateral inversion is when:", options: ["The image is upside down", "Left and right are swapped — your left hand appears as the image's right hand", "The image is smaller", "The image is blurry"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Plane mirrors cause lateral inversion — your right side appears on the left in the mirror. That's why text appears reversed."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c047_q4", text: "The image distance in a plane mirror equals:", options: ["Twice the object distance", "Half the object distance", "The same as the object distance (behind the mirror)", "Zero — the image is at the mirror surface"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "For plane mirrors: object distance (in front) = image distance (behind) — the image appears as far behind the mirror as you stand in front."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c047_q5", text: "Why is 'AMBULANCE' written in reverse on the vehicle's bonnet?", options: ["It is a decoration", "So drivers ahead see the correct word in their rear-view mirrors (lateral inversion)", "It is written in a foreign language", "To make it harder to read"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "AMBULANCE is written backwards — when seen in a car's rear-view mirror (which laterally inverts it), it reads correctly, alerting drivers to move aside.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c048: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c048_q1", text: "A pinhole camera forms an image that is:", options: ["Upright and same size", "Inverted (upside down) and smaller than the object", "Inverted and the same size", "Always larger than the object"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Light from the top of an object travels through the pinhole to the bottom of the screen (and vice versa) — forming a smaller, inverted image."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c048_q2", text: "Making the pinhole in a pinhole camera smaller makes the image:", options: ["Blurrier but brighter", "Sharper but dimmer — less light enters through a smaller hole", "Larger", "Same quality as before"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A smaller pinhole allows less but more directed light — creating a sharper image. But less light = dimmer. There is a trade-off."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c048_q3", text: "A pinhole camera works because:", options: ["The hole acts as a lens", "Light travels in straight lines — each point of the object sends rays through the hole to a unique point on the screen", "The hole bends light to form an image", "Light reflects inside the box"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Rectilinear propagation means each point on the object sends light in straight lines through the pinhole to a specific spot on the screen — forming a precise inverted image."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c048_q4", text: "If the pinhole camera box is made longer, the image:", options: ["Gets smaller", "Becomes blurrier", "Gets larger (image is projected further from the hole)", "Disappears"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "A longer box means the screen is farther from the pinhole — since light continues in straight lines, the image spreads over a larger area, making it bigger."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c048_q5", text: "The screen inside a pinhole camera must be:", options: ["Transparent", "Coloured", "Translucent or white to receive and display the image", "Metallic and reflective"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "The screen must be able to receive and show the image — translucent material (like tracing paper) or a white surface works, allowing light to form a visible image.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c049: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c049_q1", text: "A periscope is used to:", options: ["Focus sunlight for heating", "See over or around obstacles using two angled mirrors", "Measure distances", "Refract light into a spectrum"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A periscope uses two mirrors at 45° to reflect light around a corner — used in submarines, trenches and over crowds."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c049_q2", text: "In a periscope, the mirrors are placed at an angle of:", options: ["90°", "30°", "45° to the horizontal", "60°"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Mirrors at 45° to the incoming light reflect it exactly 90° (downward or around) — allowing the viewer to see horizontally what is above or around an obstacle."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c049_q3", text: "A kaleidoscope creates beautiful patterns using:", options: ["Coloured glass lenses", "Multiple reflections from mirrors arranged at specific angles", "Light refraction through water", "Shadows of coloured objects"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A kaleidoscope has two or three mirrors at 60° (or other angles) — coloured objects at one end are reflected many times, creating symmetric patterns."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c049_q4", text: "The multiple symmetrical patterns in a kaleidoscope increase as:", options: ["The tube gets longer", "More mirrors are added or mirror angles are decreased", "Fewer coloured pieces are added", "The light source gets brighter"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "More mirrors create more reflections — and smaller angles between mirrors produce more images, creating more complex symmetric patterns."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c049_q5", text: "Periscopes are used in submarines to:", options: ["Navigate using GPS", "See above the water surface while the submarine remains submerged", "Measure water depth", "Communicate with other ships"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "A submarine's periscope extends above water — light enters at the top, reflects through the periscope system, and the crew views from below.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c050: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c050_q1", text: "Day and night on Earth are caused by:", options: ["The Sun moving around Earth", "Earth's revolution around the Sun", "Earth's rotation on its own axis (west to east)", "The Moon's movement"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Earth spins on its axis every 24 hours — the side facing the Sun has day, the side facing away has night."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c050_q2", text: "Earth's axis is tilted at approximately:", options: ["0° (perfectly upright)", "45°", "23.5° from the vertical", "90° (lying flat)"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Earth's axis tilts at ~23.5° — this tilt (not the distance from the Sun) causes seasons as Earth orbits the Sun."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c050_q3", text: "The Sun appears to rise in the east because:", options: ["The Sun moves from east to west", "Earth rotates from west to east, making the Sun appear to rise in the east", "The Moon pushes the Sun eastward", "Gravity pulls the Sun eastward"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Earth rotates west to east — so as your location rotates into the Sun's light, the Sun appears to rise in the east and set in the west."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c050_q4", text: "One rotation of Earth on its axis takes:", options: ["One year", "One month", "Approximately 24 hours (one day)", "One week"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Earth completes one full rotation in approximately 24 hours — this defines the length of our day."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c050_q5", text: "If the Earth stopped rotating, what would happen?", options: ["Seasons would change faster", "One side of Earth would have permanent day, the other permanent night", "Days would become shorter", "Nothing would change for life"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Without rotation, one hemisphere would always face the Sun (extreme heat) and the other would be in permanent darkness (extreme cold) — catastrophic for life.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c051: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c051_q1", text: "Seasons are caused by:", options: ["Earth's varying distance from the Sun", "Earth's axial tilt as it revolves around the Sun", "The Moon's gravitational pull", "Sunspot activity"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Earth's tilted axis means different hemispheres receive more direct sunlight at different times of year — creating seasons as Earth orbits the Sun."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c051_q2", text: "One revolution of Earth around the Sun takes:", options: ["24 hours", "One month", "Approximately 365.25 days (one year)", "100 years"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "Earth completes one orbit (revolution) around the Sun in 365.25 days — we add a leap day every 4 years to account for the extra 0.25 days."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c051_q3", text: "When it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere, it is:", options: ["Also summer in the Southern Hemisphere", "Winter in the Southern Hemisphere", "Spring everywhere", "Autumn in the Northern Hemisphere"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "Earth's tilt means when the Northern Hemisphere leans toward the Sun (summer), the Southern Hemisphere leans away (winter) — opposite seasons."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c051_q4", text: "The summer solstice in the Northern Hemisphere (around June 21) is when:", options: ["Day and night are equal length", "The Northern Hemisphere has its shortest day", "The Northern Hemisphere has its longest day — the Sun is highest in the sky at noon", "Earth is farthest from the Sun"], correctIndex: 2, explanation: "At the June solstice, the Northern Hemisphere is tilted most toward the Sun — it receives the most direct sunlight and has the longest day of the year."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c051_q5", text: "At the equinoxes (around March 21 and Sept 23), day and night are:", options: ["Unequal everywhere", "Equal (12 hours each) everywhere on Earth", "Shortest at the equator", "Longest at the poles"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "At equinoxes, Earth's axis is perpendicular to the Sun's rays — all places receive equal sunlight, so day and night are each 12 hours globally.")
    ]

    private static let sci7_c052: [McqQuestion] = [
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c052_q1", text: "A solar eclipse occurs when:", options: ["Earth moves between the Sun and Moon", "The Moon moves between Earth and the Sun, blocking sunlight from reaching Earth", "The Sun moves away from Earth", "Earth's shadow falls on the Moon"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "In a solar eclipse, the Moon aligns between the Sun and Earth — the Moon's shadow falls on part of Earth, blocking sunlight in that area."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c052_q2", text: "A lunar eclipse occurs when:", options: ["The Moon moves in front of the Sun", "Earth moves between the Sun and Moon — Earth's shadow falls on the Moon", "The Sun becomes dimmer temporarily", "The Moon moves away from Earth"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "In a lunar eclipse, Earth is between the Sun and the Moon — Earth's shadow covers the Moon, causing it to appear dark or reddish ('blood moon')."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c052_q3", text: "A total solar eclipse is seen only in:", options: ["The entire hemisphere facing the Moon", "A narrow path (umbra) where the Moon completely blocks the Sun", "Countries near the equator only", "Both hemispheres simultaneously"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The Moon's umbra (total shadow) is narrow — only people in this path see totality. Those in the penumbra (partial shadow) see a partial eclipse."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c052_q4", text: "Why doesn't a solar eclipse happen every month despite the Moon orbiting Earth monthly?", options: ["The Moon moves too fast", "The Moon's orbit is tilted ~5° from Earth's orbit — it often passes above or below the Sun-Earth line", "The Sun is too bright", "The Moon is too far away every month"], correctIndex: 1, explanation: "The Moon's orbit is inclined ~5° relative to Earth's orbit around the Sun — it usually passes above or below the Sun from Earth's perspective, so alignment is rare."),
        McqQuestion(id: "sci7_c052_q5", text: "Which type of eclipse is safe to view with the naked eye throughout?", options: ["Total solar eclipse", "Partial solar eclipse", "Annular solar eclipse", "Lunar eclipse"], correctIndex: 3, explanation: "A lunar eclipse is safe to watch without protection — you're looking at the Moon (which reflects dim, scattered sunlight), not directly at the Sun.")
    ]

